    "content": "The Masters uses a sudden-death playoff format if golfers are tied after 72 holes. Players immediately play the 18th hole, and if still tied, proceed to the 10th hole, alternating between the two until a winner is decided. This format, adopted in 1976, has been used 12 times since 1979.This video explains the sudden-death playoff format at the Masters:58sHD SHORTSYouTube• Apr 13, 2025Hole Rotation: 18th hole →right arrow→ 10th hole →right arrow→ 18th hole....Sudden-Death Rule: The first player to win a hole outright (i.e., achieves a better score than the opponent on that specific hole) is declared the champion.History: The last playoff occurred in 2025, when Rory McIlroy defeated Justin Rose.Past Formats: Previously, the Masters used an 18-hole playoff on Monday, and before that, a 36-hole playoff.No sudden-death playoff in Masters history has ever gone beyond two holes.",
